                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Passing to a subsequence, we assume  $ (a_j, b_j) \\to (a, b) $. If  $ a = 0 $, then  $ |b| = 1 $, which is impossible. If  $ a \\neq 0 $, then for the hyperplane  $ H = \\{y : a \\cdot y + b = 0\\} $ property (5) gives a small average fraction of time in  $ U_\\eta(H) $ for sufficiently small  $ \\eta > 0 $, while outside this neighborhood  $ |a \\cdot \\Phi^t(x) + b| \\geq \\|a\\|\\eta $. This yields a positive lower bound for the limit inferior of the mean square, a contradiction. Hence (2), and therefore (6), holds.",
            "block_bbox": [
                136,
                352,
                1084,
                489
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
